---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../styles/global.css";

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Restaurant",
  name: "Metropole",
  servesCuisine: ["American", "Contemporary", "Seasonal"],
  priceRange: "$$",
  telephone: "+1-513-578-6660",
  url: "http://metropoleonwalnut.com/",
  address: {
    "@type": "PostalAddress",
    streetAddress: "609 Walnut St",
    addressLocality: "Cincinnati",
    addressRegion: "OH",
    postalCode: "45202",
    addressCountry: "US",
  },
  description:
    "Restaurant and bar inside the 21c Museum Hotel in downtown Cincinnati, serving seasonal, locally sourced American cuisine amid rotating contemporary art.",
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Metropole Menu Guide (2026) — Prices & What to Order"
  description="An independent guide to Metropole at the 21c Museum Hotel in Cincinnati: seasonal menu highlights, reported price bands, our picks for what to order, and visiting tips. Prices reported as of October 2026."
  path="/metropole-menu/"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  ogImage="/images/metropole-dining.webp"
>
  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6">
    <img
      src="/images/metropole-dining.webp"
      alt="Illustration of Metropole's art-filled restaurant dining room in Cincinnati"
      class="w-full h-64 sm:h-96 object-cover rounded-2xl mt-8"
      fetchpriority="high"
    />

    <p class="mt-8 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-gold-dark">
      Menu Guide · Downtown Cincinnati, Ohio
    </p>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl sm:text-5xl font-bold mt-2 leading-tight">
      Metropole at 21c Museum Hotel — Menu Guide
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-ink-soft">By <span class="font-semibold text-ink">Firoz Khan</span> <span class="text-gold-dark">&middot;</span> FK Digital Media</p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-ink-soft leading-relaxed">
      Metropole is the restaurant inside the 21c Museum Hotel at 609 Walnut Street in downtown
      Cincinnati — a dining room where contemporary art is as present as the food. The concept
      is straightforward: local, seasonal American cooking, an evolving cocktail and wine list,
      and a room that doubles as part of the hotel's gallery space. It earned an OpenTable
      Diners' Choice 2024 nod. Below is our independent walkthrough of the menu, reported
      prices, and what we'd order. Everything is written in our own words; prices are reported
      figures, not a copy of the restaurant's menu.
    </p>

    <!-- At a glance -->
    <div class="mt-8 rounded-2xl border border-line bg-white p-6 sm:p-8">
      <h2 class="!mt-0 font-display text-xl font-bold">At a glance</h2>
      <dl class="mt-4 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2 text-sm">
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Location</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">609 Walnut St, Cincinnati, OH 45202 (inside the 21c Museum Hotel)</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Chef</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">Corey Cova</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Cuisine</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">Seasonal American, local sourcing</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Price level</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">$31–$50 per person (OpenTable); moderately priced</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Reservations</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">Recommended for dinner; book via OpenTable or the restaurant's site</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Phone</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">+1 (513) 578-6660</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-guide">
      <h2>Starters &amp; small plates</h2>
      <p>
        The opening of the menu sets the tone: approachable, seasonal, and built for sharing.
        Reported prices for starters sit roughly in the <strong>$10–$22</strong> band, per diner
        reports. Standbys include a beet salad, octopus with grits, burnt carrot salad, and spicy
        tuna served over crispy rice — the kind of dish that reads like a small-plate menu but
        anchors a table.
      </p>
      <p>
        Our take: the spicy tuna crispy rice is the most talked-about small plate here, and the
        one that best matches the room's playful mood.
      </p>

      <h2>Pasta &amp; mains</h2>
      <p>
        The main courses lean into comfort with a chef's touch. Reported entrée prices land
        roughly in the <strong>$25–$40</strong> range. Dishes that recur in reviews include
        cavatelli cacio e pepe, a bison burger, roasted duck breast, steak au poivre, and a
        jackfruit barbecue shrimp preparation for the plant-forward crowd. The sweet potato
        pound cake is the dessert name that keeps coming up.
      </p>

      <h2>Brunch</h2>
      <p>
        Brunch runs Saturday and Sunday from 7am to 2pm — a generous window that makes it one of
        the more relaxed downtown weekend options. Weekday breakfast (Monday–Friday, 7am–11am)
        serves the hotel crowd and locals alike.
      </p>

      <h2>Hours</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Breakfast:</strong> Monday–Friday, 7am–11am</li>
        <li><strong>Brunch:</strong> Saturday–Sunday, 7am–2pm</li>
        <li><strong>Dinner:</strong> Wednesday–Saturday, 5pm–10pm</li>
        <li><strong>Lounge:</strong> Wednesday–Thursday, 4pm–10:30pm; Friday–Saturday, 4pm–11:30pm</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Note that dinner is only served Wednesday through Saturday — plan accordingly if you're
        visiting early in the week.
      </p>

      <h2>What we'd order</h2>
      <p>If we had one meal here, this is how we'd play it:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>To start:</strong> the spicy tuna crispy rice — it's the dish that defines the menu's energy.</li>
        <li><strong>The main event:</strong> roasted duck breast if you're in the mood for classic technique, or the bison burger if you want the crowd-pleaser.</li>
        <li><strong>Don't skip:</strong> the sweet potato pound cake — an unusual dessert that reviewers single out.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Planning your visit</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Go early and look around.</strong> The 21c's contemporary art carries into the restaurant — arrive before your reservation to take it in.</li>
        <li><strong>Dinner is a four-night affair</strong> (Wed–Sat). For Sunday through Tuesday evenings, brunch or the lounge are your options.</li>
        <li><strong>Budget:</strong> at $31–$50 per person, this is mid-range downtown dining — a comfortable dinner for two lands well under the fine-dining tier.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- Price disclaimer -->
    <div class="mt-10 rounded-xl border border-gold/40 bg-gold/10 p-5 text-sm text-ink-soft leading-relaxed">
      <p class="font-semibold text-ink mb-1">About these prices</p>
      <p>
        Prices above are <strong>reported as of October 2026</strong>, compiled from the restaurant's
        official site and diner reports. The menu is seasonal and prices move — always verify current
        prices with the restaurant before visiting.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-8 text-sm text-ink-soft">
      <a href="/best-hotel-restaurants-ohio/" class="text-gold-dark font-semibold hover:underline">← Back to Ohio hotel restaurants</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
